{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q015_artifact_tag_not_homology","answer":"Verified SCOPe 2.08 facts: d1a14h2 (sunid 343393, region 1a14 H:1-8) is classified in class l 'Artifacts' (sunid 310555) > fold l.1 'Tags' (310573) > superfamily l.1.1 'Tags' (310607) > family l.1.1.1 'Tags' (310682) > protein 'N-terminal Tags' (310894) > species 'Synthetic' (311501), sccs l.1.1.1. It is therefore the eight-residue N-terminal synthetic expression/purification tag segment, not a biological domain of the antibody. The remainder of the chain, d1a14h1 (sunid 20061, region H:9-111), is the genuine protein domain: class b (All beta proteins) > fold b.1 (Immunoglobulin-like beta-sandwich) > superfamily b.1.1 (Immunoglobulin) > family b.1.1.1 (V set domains, antibody variable domain-like) > protein 'Immunoglobulin heavy chain variable domain, VH' > species Mouse (Mus musculus), cluster 3.2; its annotation notes it is part of Fab NC10 with only Fv coordinates included.

Should similarity to d1a14h2 be treated as homology evidence? No. The homology section for d1a14h2 reports homology_implied_by_level = false and homology_scope = null, with the explicit warning 'artifact_not_homology_evidence' (severity caution): class l contains artificial sequence artifacts and similarity to these records does not imply biological homology. Although class l is organized with fold/superfamily/family nodes for bookkeeping, those levels in class l carry none of the evolutionary meaning that family (close relationship) and superfamily (probable common ancestry) carry in the ordinary classes a-g; grouping of tags into l.1.1.1 reflects shared synthetic origin, not descent from a common ancestral protein. A BLAST or FATCAT hit from any query to d1a14h2 or any other l.1.1.1 tag domain should be discarded or flagged as an artifact match, and no family, superfamily, fold, or functional inference should be transferred from it. Such hits are also unsafe as benchmark positives or negatives and should be excluded or stratified in dataset design.

Limits: by contrast, d1a14h1 has homology_implied_by_level = true with no warnings, so matches to the H:9-111 segment can be interpreted at the Immunoglobulin superfamily / V-set family level in the ordinary way; however, that still only supports common ancestry, not a specific antigen specificity or function, and whole-chain comparisons should be done against d1a14h1 rather than the full chain so the tag does not create spurious N-terminal similarity. The exact tag sequence was not retrieved here (it would require the domain sequence endpoint), but its identity as a synthetic N-terminal tag is established by the retrieved lineage.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/biological-interpretation.md","mcp__scope__get_scop_domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1a14h1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1a14h1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1a14h1/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1a14h1/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1a14h2","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1a14h2/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1a14h2/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1a14h2/homology"]}]}
